<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-exponential" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exponential Functions</title>

<p>Exponential functions have the form <m>a^x</m>, where a is called the base. In this course we'll be dealing with situations where <m>x</m> is an integer. 
Much like <m>3^2=3\times 3</m> and <m>4^3=4\times 4\times 4</m>, if we have <m>2^x</m> we are multiplying
<m>x</m> copies of 2.</p>

<p> There are a few exponent rules that you will need to be familiar with. The first ones are for multiplying and dividing exponential terms with the same base:</p>
<md>a^xa^y=a^{x+y},</md>
<md>\frac{a^x}{a^y}=a^{x-y}.</md>



<p>We also need to know what to do when there are multiple powers. In particular,</p>
<md>(a^x)^y=a^{xy}=(a^y)^x.</md>

<p>Finally, if two exponential functions with the same base are equal, then the powers must be equal, so if <m>a^x=a^y</m>, then <m>x=y</m>.</p>

<example>
    <title>Solving Exponential Equations</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Solve the equation <m>2^{2x-1}4^x=8^x</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The rules above required the bases to be the same. However, note that <m>4=2^2</m> and <m>8=2^3</m>. If we make those replacements, then we will obtain the same base in all three terms: </p>
      <md>2^{2x-1}(2^2)^x=(2^3)^x.</md>
      <p>Now we can use the rule regarding powers of powers to get</p>
      <md>2^{2x-1}2^{2x}=2^{3x}.</md>
      <p>Now the left-hand-side has a product of two exponential functions with the same base, so we may add the powers:</p>
      <md>2^{(2x-1)+2x}=2^{3x}.</md>
      <p>From here, we know that the powers must be equal, so</p>
      <md>(2x-1)+2x=3x.</md>
      <p>This is a linear equation, which we can solve:</p>
      <md>4x-1=3x,</md>
      <md>x=1.</md>

    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise label="ww-exp">
    <title>Solving Exponential Equations</title>
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $x = 3;
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <m>16^x=2^{5-x}4^{x+2}</m>.
            </p>   
            <p><m>x=</m><var name="$x" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>

<subsection>
  <title>Exercises</title>


<exercise label="ww-exp2">
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $x = 4;
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <m>9^x=3^{x+4}</m>.
            </p>   
            <p><m>x=</m><var name="$x" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>

<exercise label="ww-exp3">
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $x = 2;
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <m>5^{2-x}5^{2x}=25^x</m>.
            </p>   
            <p><m>x=</m><var name="$x" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>

<exercise label="ww-exp4">
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $x = 5;
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <m>4^{7-x}16^x=2^{5x-1}</m>.
            </p>   
            <p><m>x=</m><var name="$x" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>


</subsection>
</section>